units={
'WORD-CLASSES':('Word classes and noun categories',['Distinguish common/proper and concrete/abstract nouns.','Identify pronouns, lexical verbs, auxiliaries and adjectives in context.'],'''A word's class depends on its job in a sentence. In “They paint the gate”, paint is a verb; in “The paint is wet”, paint is a noun. Nouns name people, places, things or ideas. A common noun names a category (city); a proper noun identifies a particular name (Ibadan). Concrete and abstract describe a different distinction: a stone is a material object; patience is a quality. Common is not the opposite of concrete.
Count nouns can be counted: one notebook, three notebooks. In ordinary usage, information and equipment are non-count: say “two pieces of information” and “some equipment”, not “informations”. Some nouns have count and non-count uses: chicken can name an animal or food. Learn irregular plurals from a dictionary: child/children, mouse/mice, parenthesis/parentheses, deer/deer. A compound usually pluralises its head: mother-in-law/mothers-in-law; head of state/heads of state.
Pronouns can replace a noun phrase: “Bola owns the bag. She carries it.” Use she as subject and her as object. Possessive determiners precede nouns (her bag); possessive pronouns stand alone (hers). An adjective describes a noun: a quiet room; the room is quiet. A lexical verb expresses the main event or state: “I know the answer.” Be, have and do can be lexical verbs or auxiliaries. In “She has finished”, has is auxiliary; in “She has a bicycle”, has is lexical. Modal auxiliaries such as can normally require a main verb in a complete verb phrase: can swim. Finite verbs carry tense or agreement; infinitives and participles are non-finite: to swim, swimming, written. Non-finite does not simply mean an -ing ending.
To analyse a sentence, find the main verb, ask who or what participates, then identify describing words and pronouns. Do not classify a word solely by its ending: childlike is an adjective despite ending in -like.''', [('Classify city, Ibadan, patience and stone using the distinctions taught.','city: common; Ibadan: proper; patience: abstract; stone: concrete.',['common/proper distinction','abstract/concrete distinction']),('Explain the two uses of has in “She has a pen” and “She has written”.','The first is a lexical possession verb; the second is an auxiliary with the participle written.',['lexical possession','auxiliary plus participle']),('Correct “two informations” and “three mother-in-laws”.','two pieces of information; three mothers-in-law.',['non-count correction','compound plural'])],[NOUN,CAM]),
'PHRASES-CLAUSES':('Phrases, clauses and sentence functions',['Distinguish a phrase from a finite clause.','Identify subjects, objects and complements.'],'''A phrase is a unit built around a head: “the new library” is a noun phrase headed by library. In “The new library opens today”, the whole phrase is the subject. In “We visited the new library”, it is the direct object. “We gave the librarian a book” contains indirect object the librarian and direct object a book. In “The librarian is our neighbour”, our neighbour is a subject complement. In “They elected Ayo captain”, captain is an object complement; it describes Ayo, not the subject they.
A verb phrase can contain a main verb alone or auxiliaries: works; has worked; may have been working. A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and its complement: in the room, after the lesson. Its function depends on context: “the book on the desk” modifies book, while “She studied in the room” tells where she studied.
Finite clauses contain a finite verb and usually a subject. “The bell rang” is a main clause. “when the bell rang” is subordinate. English also has non-finite clauses, so it is too strong to say every clause must have a finite verb. At this level, start by recognising finite examples. “What she wrote pleased us”: What she wrote is a noun clause functioning as subject. “We know that she arrived”: that she arrived is an object clause. “The girl who won smiled”: who won modifies girl and is a relative clause, not merely an adjective phrase. “We left because it was late”: because it was late gives a reason.
A simple sentence has one main clause; a compound has two or more coordinated main clauses; a complex sentence has a main and subordinate clause; a compound-complex combines both. Count clauses, not just the number of visible verbs: “Ada sings and dances” can have one subject and a coordinated verb phrase. Use punctuation with the grammatical structure: “It rained, but we stayed” differs from the comma splice “It rained, we stayed”.''',[('Identify the objects in “Bisi gave her brother a notebook”.','her brother is indirect object; a notebook is direct object.',['indirect object','direct object']),('What does “who won” do in “The girl who won smiled”?','It is a relative clause modifying girl.',['relative clause','modifies girl']),('Classify “We left because it was late” and explain.','Complex: one main clause plus a subordinate reason clause.',['complex','reasoned clause analysis'])],[CAM]),
'AFFIXES':('Affixes and reliable word formation',['Distinguish a base, prefix and suffix.','Form words without treating accidental letter sequences as affixes.'],'''A base is the form to which an affix is added. A prefix precedes it: un + kind gives unkind. A suffix follows it: kind + ness gives kindness. Some affixes change meaning, some change word class, and some mark grammar. Unkind is still an adjective; kindness is a noun. Books adds a plural ending; walked adds a past-tense ending. Do not confuse derivational endings such as -en in widen with a past-tense ending.
Useful prefixes include re- (again: rewrite), pre- (before: preview), post- (after: postgraduate), anti- (against: antisocial), inter- (between: interschool), and bi- (two: bilingual). The negative prefix varies: legal/illegal, possible/impossible, regular/irregular, correct/incorrect. These patterns do not license inventing a form for every word. Check a dictionary when unsure. Antenatal begins with ante-, meaning before; anti- is different. An apparent shared spelling is not sufficient evidence of the same root: comedy is not come plus a suffix.
Suffix examples are teach/teacher, enjoy/enjoyment, happy/happiness, harm/harmless and beauty/beautiful. Spelling can change: happy loses y before -ness and gains i. A final silent e commonly drops before -ing: write/writing; it is often retained before -ment: move/movement. Learn exceptions and accepted spelling varieties. Noticeable retains e to keep the soft c sound. One-syllable consonant-vowel-consonant bases often double a final consonant before a vowel suffix: stop/stopped. Longer words depend partly on stress: admit/admitted but visit/visited. Do not apply this mechanically to w, x or y.
Analyse the intended meaning first, choose a real word, then check spelling and word class in a sentence. “She spoke clearly” needs an adverb; “Her explanation was clear” needs an adjective.''',[('Form nouns from happy and enjoy, and identify the suffix.','happiness (-ness); enjoyment (-ment).',['happiness and suffix','enjoyment and suffix']),('Correct “anti-natal”, “writeing” and “comedy is come plus -dy”.','antenatal; writing; comedy is not formed from the verb come in this way.',['ante- correction','writing','rejects false segmentation']),('Use harmless and rewrite in meaningful sentences.','Example: The mark is harmless. Please rewrite the unclear sentence.',['harmless used appropriately','rewrite used appropriately'])],[CAM]),
'PHONETICS':('English sound symbols and listening contrasts',['Read a consistent phonemic notation.','Distinguish selected consonant and vowel contrasts without confusing letters with sounds.'],'''This lesson uses the conventional British teaching chart as a reference model, not a claim that all English accents sound identical. A phoneme symbol represents a sound; a spelling may represent different sounds in different words. Slashes mark a phonemic transcription. The length mark is ː, not an ordinary spelling colon.
The chart's pure vowels are /iː ɪ e æ ɑː ɒ ɔː ʊ uː ʌ ɜː ə/. Familiar examples include fleece, kit, dress, trap, palm, lot, thought, foot, goose, strut, nurse and the final sound of comma. A diphthong moves between vowel qualities: /eɪ/ day, /aɪ/ my, /ɔɪ/ boy, /aʊ/ now, /əʊ/ go, /ɪə/ near, /eə/ square and the traditional chart's /ʊə/ cure. Some modern accents merge or realise the last three differently; consult the same reference model throughout an exercise.
Voicing contrasts include /p b/, /t d/, /k g/, /f v/, /θ ð/, /s z/, /ʃ ʒ/ and /tʃ dʒ/. The remaining consonants are /m n ŋ h l r w j/. /t/ is the voiceless alveolar stop in ten; /k/ is the velar stop in cat. /j/ as in yes is voiced. /ŋ/ occurs at the end of sing; it is not the letter j. /ʒ/ occurs in measure and /dʒ/ in judge. Do not substitute a Cyrillic look-alike for these IPA characters.
Compare sip/zip and fine/vine; only the initial phoneme changes in each pair. Compare ship/sheep for /ɪ/ and /iː/: quality as well as duration matters. Two words rhyme when their final stressed vowel and the following sounds agree, as in light/night. Ride/site do not rhyme because /d/ and /t/ differ.
Listen to an authorised model, repeat, record yourself if permitted, and compare. Written answers establish symbol recognition, not certified pronunciation. The teacher's oral observation remains separate.''',[('Correct these descriptions: /k/ in ten; /j/ is voiceless; ride and site rhyme.','ten begins /t/; /j/ is voiced; ride and site have different final consonants.',['/t/','voiced /j/','rhyme correction']),('Give the contrasts in sip/zip and ship/sheep.','/s/ versus /z/; /ɪ/ versus /iː/.',['consonant pair','vowel pair']),('Describe a responsible way to check pronunciation.','Listen to a consistent authorised model and obtain oral feedback; a written quiz alone cannot certify speech.',['consistent model','oral limitation'])],[IPA,BC]),
'READING':('Reading details, inference, scanning and summary with an original passage',['Locate facts and make supported inferences.','Distinguish skimming from scanning.','Summarise without inventing facts.'],'''This is a newly authored equivalent activity, not a reproduction of a missing textbook passage or a past examination.
Passage — The library timetable
The school library used to open only during the lunch break. Mariam noticed that students who helped serve lunch often arrived just as the door closed. She counted visits for five days and asked classmates when they could attend. Their answers suggested that a short period after lessons would help. The librarian agreed to try two afternoon sessions, on Tuesday and Thursday, for three weeks. Students had to return borrowed books by the following Monday. During the trial, visits increased, but the room became noisy near the entrance. Two volunteers moved the returns box away from the reading tables and placed a clear sign beside it. At the end of the trial, the librarian kept the afternoon sessions and asked the volunteers to continue recording visits. Mariam did not claim that every problem had been solved; she recommended checking whether students who travelled far could also benefit.
Skim by looking quickly for the overall topic and purpose. Here, the passage describes a trial that made a library more accessible. Scan for a particular fact: the sessions were Tuesday and Thursday, not every afternoon. Then read closely to understand relationships. The phrase “just as the door closed” explains why lunchtime access failed for some students. It is reasonable to infer that the old timetable was inconvenient for lunch helpers; it is not reasonable to infer that they disliked reading.
For context vocabulary, substitute a candidate meaning and reread: trial means a temporary test here, not a court proceeding. Main ideas explain the problem, response and result. A summary leaves out illustrative details while preserving these relationships. In two sentences: “The school tested afternoon library sessions to help pupils who could not attend at lunchtime. Visits increased, and the librarian retained the sessions while arranging further monitoring.” This is a model answer for this activity, not an examination board's universal marking rule. Follow the word or sentence limit given in each question.''',[('Which days had afternoon sessions, and when were books due?','Tuesday and Thursday; the following Monday.',['both days','return date']),('Why is “the students disliked reading” an unsupported inference?','The passage reports inconvenient opening times, not dislike of reading.',['identifies timetable evidence','rejects unsupported motive']),('Explain trial in context and summarise the outcome in one sentence.','Trial means a temporary test. Example summary: Afternoon access increased visits and was retained with continued monitoring.',['context meaning','accurate concise outcome'])],[OWL]),
'ESSAY-TYPES':('Planning four essay types',['Distinguish narrative, descriptive, expository and argumentative purposes.','Build a coherent paragraph and an evidence-based argument.'],'''An essay's purpose determines its organisation. A narrative recounts linked events, usually with a setting, participants, a difficulty, a turning point and an outcome. Choose a consistent viewpoint and use time links rather than a list of unrelated events. A descriptive essay creates a precise impression of a person, place or event. Select relevant sensory details and arrange them spatially or by importance; description need not invent facts about a real person.
An expository essay explains a subject or process. Define important terms, organise the explanation into stages or reasons, and illustrate it. For “How our class can reduce paper waste”, a plan could explain the problem, introduce shared drafts, explain reusing one-sided sheets, and discuss monitoring results. An argumentative essay advances a defensible position. State a claim, supply reasons and examples, consider a counterargument, and reach a conclusion. A claim is not proved merely by repeating it. Avoid making invented statistics look like evidence.
Model argumentative paragraph: “A quiet reading period should remain on the timetable because it gives students uninterrupted practice. For example, pupils can finish a short text and discuss its main idea afterwards. Some may prefer free conversation, but that can be scheduled separately. A short protected period balances both needs.” The example is hypothetical, not a research result.
Write a plan before drafting. Each paragraph should develop one point with explanation; link paragraphs so the reader can follow the sequence. Revise for relevance, clarity, grammar and spelling. A proverb-based story may illustrate or question a saying: it need not endorse discrimination. Describe a character's belief as that character's belief, not a universal truth about men or women.''',[('Choose a suitable type for explaining a process and for recounting an incident.','Expository for a process; narrative for an incident.',['expository','narrative']),('Give a claim, counterargument and response about a school reading period.','Example: Keep a reading period; some prefer conversation; timetable both activities separately.',['clear claim','fair counterargument','reasoned response']),('Write an 80–120-word paragraph explaining one way to reduce paper waste.','Rubric: a clear method, explanation of how it works, a relevant example and coherent language. Different workable methods are acceptable.',['clear method','explanation','example','coherence'])],[CAM,OWL]),
'ADVERBS-TENSE':('Adverbs and tense in context',['Use adverbs with suitable verb forms.','Distinguish simple, continuous and perfect forms.'],'''An adverb can modify a verb, adjective or another adverb: runs quickly; very careful; quite slowly. Many manner adverbs end in -ly, but fast and hard do not. Hardly usually means almost not, not “with great effort”. Well is the usual adverb corresponding to good. A sentence adverb can express the speaker's view: “Fortunately, the bus arrived.” A linking adverb connects ideas: “It rained; however, the match continued.” It is not punctuated like the coordinating conjunction but.
The simple present expresses habits or general situations: “She reads after school.” The present continuous uses am/is/are plus -ing for an ongoing or temporary activity: “She is reading now.” The simple past places an event in a finished past period: “She read yesterday.” The present perfect uses has/have plus a past participle and relates a past event or continuing situation to the present: “She has finished”; “She has lived here for two years.” It is not restricted to things that have just happened. The past perfect uses had plus a past participle for an earlier past event: “She had left before the rain began.” The present perfect continuous uses has/have been plus -ing: “She has been reading for an hour.”
Choose the form from the intended time relationship. In ordinary usage, “She has finished yesterday” conflicts with the finished past time; use “She finished yesterday.” A phrase such as for two years can fit different tenses depending on whether the period is continuing or finished. Do not decide from one cue word alone.''',[('Correct “She has finished yesterday” and “He worked hardly” when he made a great effort.','She finished yesterday. He worked hard.',['past tense','hard not hardly']),('Explain “She had left before the rain began”.','Leaving happened earlier than the beginning of the rain; had left is past perfect.',['event order','past perfect']),('Write one habitual and one ongoing sentence using read.','She reads every evening. She is reading now.',['habitual form','ongoing form'])],[CAM]),
'MEANING':('Synonyms, antonyms and contextual choice',['Choose a synonym that preserves meaning in context.','Distinguish gradable and complementary opposites.'],'''Near-synonyms overlap in meaning but are not interchangeable everywhere. A portrait represents a person; a sketch may be a quick outline; a print is a reproduced image. Paint is the colouring material or an action, not the name for a printed copy. In “a bright pupil”, bright can mean intelligent; in “a bright lamp”, it concerns light. Substitute a candidate, check grammar and then check the intended sense. Reject and emit are not general synonyms.
Antonyms express an opposition in a particular sense. Gradable opposites admit intermediate values: hot/cold with warm between them. Comparative forms such as hotter express degree; they are not the definition of gradable antonymy. Complementary opposites divide a stated domain into mutually exclusive alternatives, such as present/absent on a simple attendance register. Real-world categories may need qualifications, so do not treat married/single as an exhaustive description of everyone's status. Converse terms express a relationship from different viewpoints: buy/sell, parent/child.
Affixes sometimes form opposites: possible/impossible, legal/illegal, kind/unkind. The choice is conventional; do not invent a prefix for every word. Use a dictionary for an unfamiliar sense and check a complete sentence.''',[('Explain why bright cannot always be replaced with intelligent.','Bright describes light in some contexts and intelligence in others; only the latter sense permits the substitution.',['two senses','context restriction']),('Give a gradable pair and a converse pair.','hot/cold; buy/sell.',['gradable example','converse example']),('Correct “a paint is a copied picture” and “reject means emit”.','A print can be a reproduced picture; reject means refuse or dismiss in relevant contexts, not generally emit.',['print distinction','reject distinction'])],[CAM]),
'LETTERS':('Writing a respectful informal letter',['Organise an informal letter.','Give advice without pressuring another person.'],'''An informal letter is written to someone with whom the writer has a personal relationship. Include an address and date where the task requires them, a suitable greeting, an opening that establishes the purpose, a developed body and an appropriate close such as “With best wishes” followed by your name. A precise examination format must come from the actual task rather than an invented universal rule.
Advice should respect the recipient's agency. If a fictional relative faces a difficult relationship decision, acknowledge the difficulty, avoid blame, and suggest trusted support. Do not tell someone to remain in an unsafe situation. The language-learning objective is coherent, considerate communication; it does not require the writer to endorse a harmful decision.
Model opening: “Dear Sade, Thank you for telling me how you feel. I am sorry this has been difficult. I hope you can talk with someone you trust and consider what is safe and right for you.” A second paragraph can offer practical help without making the decision for the recipient. Close warmly and check that every paragraph contributes to the purpose.''',[('List four structural parts of an informal letter.','Greeting, opening/purpose, developed body and personal close; address/date are also acceptable where required.',['greeting','opening','body','close']),('Rewrite “You must stay whatever happens” as considerate advice.','Example: Please put your safety first and seek support from someone you trust while deciding what to do.',['respects agency','prioritises safety']),('Write a short letter encouraging a friend preparing for an examination.','Accept a relevant friendly opening, two practical suggestions and an appropriate close, expressed coherently.',['relevant opening','practical suggestions','close','coherence'])],[CAM]),
'STRESS':('Word stress, intonation and dictionary use',['Locate stress with a consistent dictionary model.','Explain how intonation changes pragmatic meaning.'],'''Word stress is prominence within a word. It can be marked by ˈ before a stressed syllable in IPA. Some noun/verb pairs shift stress, such as a REcord and to reCORD, but this is not a rule for every two-syllable word. Consent does not automatically shift because its word class changes. Check a reliable pronunciation dictionary and listen to the same variety used in class.
Sentence prominence highlights information: “BOLA borrowed the book” contrasts the borrower; “Bola BORROWED the book” contrasts borrowing with another action. In a response question, choose the alternative that contrasts the highlighted element rather than changing an unrelated fact.
Intonation is movement of pitch across an utterance. Questions and requests do not all have one compulsory tune. “Could you open the window, please?” may use a polite rise or a fall-rise depending on context. A direct command such as “Get out” is not automatically a polite request. Teach recognition through a model recording and actual interaction, not a blank printed arrow. A dictionary entry can show spelling, word class, pronunciation, meanings and examples. The apostrophe-like stress mark and syllable divisions must be read carefully; spelling alone cannot supply stress.
For practice, choose a word, read its pronunciation entry, listen, mark the stressed syllable, then use it in a sentence. Written identification and oral production are different assessment outcomes.''',[('Why is “all two-syllable nouns stress the first syllable” unsafe as a rule?','It overgeneralises; stress is lexical and exceptions require dictionary/model checks.',['rejects universal rule','uses lexical evidence']),('What contrast is suggested by “BOLA borrowed the book”?','Bola rather than another person borrowed it.',['correct focus']),('Turn “Get out” into a considerate request in a suitable context.','Example: Could you wait outside for a moment, please? Tone and context still matter.',['polite form','context awareness'])],[BC,CAM])
}
